{title2=A16607W1}

Source paper: https://www.whatdotheyknow.com/request/ba_computer_science_past_papers/response/2577745/attach/2/202402%20183%20CS%20Papers.zip?cookie_passthrough=1[released Oxford examination archive], member `2018/T/a16607w1.pdf`. Its native cover identifies the Second Public Examination, Trinity Term 2019, Monday 3 June, 14:30–16:30. The archive directory predates the actual cover year. All three questions and every unnumbered requirement within their printed parts are addressed. The blocks-world figure is intact.

== 1
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

**The first assertion is false.** Give the root a first child that is a <goal state> of cost ten and another child leading to many cheaper non-goal <search nodes>. <Depth-first search> can take the first child and stop immediately. <A-star search> with the <admissible heuristic> $h=0$ behaves as <uniform-cost search> and expands the cheaper non-goal <search nodes> before selecting that goal. Thus an <admissible heuristic> does not make <A-star search> expand fewer nodes than every possible <depth-first search> ordering. The latter need not return an optimal solution.

**The second assertion is true under the usual finite-<branching factor> assumption.** <Breadth-first search> orders by <search depth>, not <path cost>. Every finite depth contains finitely many <search nodes>, so a goal at finite depth is eventually reached even if some steps cost zero. Zero-cost steps can affect cost optimality, but do not obstruct this completeness argument. An infinite <branching factor> would invalidate the argument irrespective of <step costs>.

**The third assertion is true as a worst-case black-box requirement, with a finite optimum assumed.** If outgoing actions are known only by expanding a state, an unexamined reachable branch can conceal a sufficiently negative-cost action followed by a route to a goal. Its total cost can beat the current solution by an arbitrarily large amount. Without additional lower bounds or structural information, finding one cheap solution cannot justify stopping; exhaustive examination can be necessary. This does not assert that every already-described irrelevant state must be visited on every input. Moreover, a reachable negative-cost <cycle in a graph> that can still reach a goal can make costs unbounded below, in which case no finite-cost optimal solution exists at all.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

<Iterative deepening depth-first search> increases a bound on the number of actions, usually through $0,1,2,\ldots$. <Iterative lengthening search> instead bounds accumulated <path cost>; after an unsuccessful pass, its next bound is the least <path cost> among the rejected <search nodes>. Thus it can distinguish shallow expensive paths from deeper cheap ones.

Both repeatedly perform <depth-first search> and retain its small active-path storage, trading repeated work for avoiding a large <search frontier>. With unit <step costs> their bounds coincide. With unequal positive <step costs>, <iterative deepening depth-first search> finds a solution with the fewest actions, while <iterative lengthening search>, under the termination conditions discussed next, finds one of least total cost. Real-valued <path costs> can make <iterative lengthening search> perform many more passes than the depth-bounded method.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

Start the threshold at zero and let $C^*$ be the least solution cost. Suppose an unsuccessful pass has threshold $L<C^*$. Along an optimal action <sequence>, take the first prefix whose <path cost> exceeds $L$. Its preceding prefix is explored, so that rejected prefix contributes to the next-threshold calculation. Positive <step costs> imply that its cost is at most $C^*$. Therefore the next threshold is also at most $C^*$.

By <induction>, no threshold used before the first solution exceeds $C^*$. A returned <goal state> therefore has cost at most $C^*$; by the definition of $C^*$ it cannot cost less. **The first returned solution is optimal.**

The termination qualification matters. In a finitely branching problem with <step costs> at least $\epsilon>0$, a threshold $L$ admits only depths up to $\lfloor L/\epsilon\rfloor$, so each pass is finite and only finitely many <path costs> precede $C^*$. These conditions establish completeness as well. Strict positivity alone on an arbitrary infinite tree is insufficient: an infinite branch with costs $1/2,1/4,1/8,\ldots$ can remain below a finite threshold and trap <depth-first search>. The optimality argument above is valid whenever the required passes terminate; a finite graph with finitely many strictly positive action costs supplies a uniform positive lower bound.

=== d
{scope}

==== Solution

With initial threshold zero and unit <step costs>, the thresholds are $0,1,\ldots,d$, so **there are $d+1$ passes**. Starting the first pass at one instead omits the zero-threshold pass when the root is not a goal.

At depth $i$, at most $b^i$ <search nodes> are admitted in each pass with threshold at least $i$. Across all passes, the full-to-cutoff visit bound is
$$
V(b,d)=\sum_{i=0}^{d}(d+1-i)b^i.
$$
This is attained when the only depth-$d$ goal is visited last. If a <goal test> occurs before expansion, the goal is visited but not expanded, giving the corresponding worst-case expansion count
$$
\boxed{E(b,d)=\sum_{i=0}^{d}(d+1-i)b^i-1.}
$$
For $b\ne1$, the visit bound is $V(b,d)=[b^{d+2}-(d+2)b+d+1]/(b-1)^2$, hence the worst-case number expanded is **$\Theta(b^d)$ for fixed $b>1$**. For $b=1$, it is $\Theta(d^2)$. An earlier goal reduces the final pass, so its exact count cannot be inferred from $b,d$ alone. Children generated and immediately rejected above a threshold are not counted as expanded; counting those generated nodes adds the corresponding branching overhead.

=== e
{scope}

==== Solution

A lower bound on individual <step costs> does **not** bound the difference between consecutive <path cost> thresholds. Two different paths can have costs differing by an arbitrarily small amount, even though every edge costs at least $\epsilon$.

Let $C^*$ be the optimal solution cost. Every unsuccessful threshold is the <path cost> of some <search node> below $C^*$. Such a node has depth strictly less than $C^*/\epsilon$. For a uniform <branching factor> $b>1$, the number of passes is therefore bounded by
$$
1+\sum_{j=0}^{\lceil C^*/\epsilon\rceil-1}b^j=O\!\left(b^{\lceil C^*/\epsilon\rceil}\right).
$$
If a solution of depth $d$ exists, each of its edges costs at most one, so $C^*\leq d$ and an associated bound is **$O(b^{\lceil d/\epsilon\rceil})$ passes**. If $d$ denotes the optimal solution's depth, the same inequality applies. With no solution-cost or solution-depth bound, $b$ and $\epsilon$ alone give no finite uniform iteration bound.

The exponential behavior is possible. Put a depth-$d$ goal on a branch with unit costs and let a large competing subtree use costs just above $\epsilon$. Choose tiny distinct perturbations so that its many <search nodes> below cost $d$ have distinct <path costs>. The threshold can advance through those costs one at a time. Thus **a bound such as $\lceil d/\epsilon\rceil$ on the number of passes is unjustified**: it bounds admitted depth, not the number of distinct costs. For $b=1$ there is only one path, so the corresponding count is linear in the admitted depth rather than exponential.

=== f
{scope}

==== Solution

For the <three-colourability problem>, represent a candidate by a complete colour assignment $c:V\to\{r,g,b\}$, initially chosen at random. Give it the conflict <objective function>
$$
H(c)=\sum_{\{u,v\}\in E}\mathbf1_{c(u)=c(v)}.
$$
A neighbor changes the colour of one <vertex> to either other colour. For <hill climbing>, maximize $-H$, or equivalently repeatedly choose a recolouring that reduces $H$. One can inspect all $2|V|$ neighbors, or focus on conflicted <vertices> and evaluate their alternative colours using only incident <edges>. Stop successfully as soon as **$H=0$**, which is exactly a <proper graph colouring> with three colours.

If no improving move exists at positive $H$, the method is stuck in a <local minimum>. Random restarts, limited sideways moves or other exploratory <local search> choices can reduce this failure mode, but do not turn a finite unsuccessful run into a proof of impossibility. On a graph with no <proper graph colouring> using three colours, every complete colouring has $H>0$, so this procedure cannot return a valid colouring. Conversely, a positive-conflict stopping point also occurs on some graphs admitting a <proper graph colouring> with three colours. **Failure of <hill climbing> alone does not certify non-colourability.** For a concrete trap, use a <bipartite graph> with three <vertices> on each side and every cross-<edge>, and give each side one vertex of each colour. There are three conflicts. Recolouring any single vertex leaves it with exactly one same-colour neighbor, so no single change improves $H$, even though colouring each whole side differently solves the instance.

=== g
{scope}

==== Solution

For an action <sequence> $\pi$, let $T_\pi(B)$ be the <set> of all physical states obtainable by applying it to the initial <belief state> $B$. Under a deterministic model this is an image; for nondeterministic outcomes it is the <union> of all permitted images. For $B'\subseteq B$,
$$
T_\pi(B')\subseteq T_\pi(B).
$$
If each action is applicable to every current possibility from $B$, it is also applicable to every possibility from $B'$. If the final <belief state> for $B$ is contained in the physical goal <set> $G$, then $T_\pi(B')\subseteq G$. Thus **the same <plan> solves every <subset> of $B$**.

Nothing comparable follows for an arbitrary <superset>. For example, let an action take $s_1$ to a goal and leave $s_2$ at a non-goal. It solves $\{s_1\}$ but not $\{s_1,s_2\}$. A larger <belief state> is solved by this particular <sequence> exactly when all added states also permit it and end in $G$. It may succeed on some <supersets> and fail on others; it cannot be guaranteed merely from success on $B$.

=== h
{scope}

==== Solution

Use <incremental belief-state search> to find only the initial possibilities needed to constrain a successful <plan>. Choose one possible initial state, or a small <subset> $B_0$ of the full initial <belief state> $B$. Find an action <sequence> solving this restricted problem, then simulate it from every state in $B$.

If validation succeeds, return the candidate. Otherwise choose a state that makes some action inapplicable or ends outside the goal, add it to $B_0$, and search again for a <plan> that works for every selected state. Replanning can revise the earlier <sequence>; merely appending a repair to one arbitrary candidate can miss solutions. For a finite initial <belief state>, there are at most $|B|$ additions before every initial possibility has been included. If a complete restricted solver proves that even the selected <subset> is unsolvable, part (g) shows that the full problem is unsolvable.

The benefits are smaller intermediate <belief states>, fewer states propagated through candidate actions, and avoidance of unnecessary portions of the full <power set> search space. One physical-state counterexample can eliminate many invalid candidates. The worst case can still require all initial states; the method exploits instances where a few representative possibilities already force a <plan> that passes universal validation.

== 2
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

Let $Q=\{1,\ldots,n\}^2$ be the board squares and let $\operatorname{Attack}(p,q)$ mean that the absolute coordinate differences are $(1,2)$ or $(2,1)$.

**One <variable> per knight.** Use $X_1,\ldots,X_k$, with each <constraint domain> equal to $Q$. For every pair $i<j$, require
$$
X_i\ne X_j,\qquad\neg\operatorname{Attack}(X_i,X_j).
$$
Every satisfying assignment places $k$ distinct nonattacking knights, and any such placement gives an assignment after labelling its knights. This has only $k$ <variables> and $O(k^2)$ <binary constraints>, but domains of size $n^2$. Relabelling the knights gives $k!$ assignments for the same unlabelled board, so the representation contains considerable redundancy.

**One <Boolean variable> per square.** Use $Y_q\in\{0,1\}$ for every $q\in Q$. Impose the <cardinality constraint>
$$
\sum_{q\in Q}Y_q=k,
$$
and for every knight-attack pair $p,q$ impose $Y_p+Y_q\leq1$. There are $n^2$ <variables>, each with a two-element <constraint domain>, and only $O(n^2)$ local attack restrictions, because each square attacks at most eight others. The single counting constraint is a global constraint, not a <binary constraint>.

The square encoding avoids knight-label redundancy and has sparse local attack relations; the knight encoding uses fewer <variables> when $k$ is small and makes single-knight moves natural. Both are exact representations of the <nonattacking knights problem>. Neither may impose one knight per row: several knights can legally share a row.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

Use the knight-variable representation and build a placement one knight at a time. The initial state is the empty placement. An action places the next knight on an empty square attacking no previously placed knight; the <search transition model> appends that square. The <goal test> is that $k$ knights have been placed. Each action adds exactly one, so any solution has depth **$k$**.

Put $N=n^2$. At depth $j$, there are at most $N-j$ choices, hence <branching factor> at most $N$. A coarse <search tree> size bound is $1+N+\cdots+N^k$. Keeping the shrinking choice count gives the sharper bound
$$
\sum_{j=0}^{k}(N)_j,\qquad (N)_j=\frac{N!}{(N-j)!},
$$
where $(N)_j$ is a <falling factorial>. This counts ordered placements before attack pruning.

For a full expansion down to depth $k$, the number of depth-$k$ leaves is at most **$(N)_k$**, or approximately $n^{2k}$ when $k$ is small relative to $N$. If $I_k$ denotes the number of legal unlabelled $k$-knight placements, the exact number of legal depth-$k$ leaves is $k!I_k$. There can also be earlier dead-end leaves: a legal placement may leave no further nonattacking square. Writing $M_j$ for the number of inclusion-maximal legal $j$-knight placements, the fully explored pruned tree has
$$
k!I_k+\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}j!M_j
$$
leaves. Its leaf count is still at most the unpruned $(N)_k$, since each early leaf replaces a disjoint nonempty subtree of the unpruned tree. <Breadth-first search> that stops at its first goal can construct less of the last layer; if no solution exists, it exhausts the reachable partial placements.

Identifying different insertion orders changes the representation. With a state that is an unlabelled <set> of occupied squares and repeated-state elimination, the corresponding depth-$k$ state bound is $\binom Nk$, with $I_k$ legal goal states. The factorial and binomial bounds refer to these different representations, not competing counts of the same tree.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

<Backtracking> assigns one <variable> at a time and abandons a partial assignment as soon as it cannot extend to a solution. With <forward checking>, placing a knight deletes its occupied and attacked squares from every remaining knight's <constraint domain>. An empty <constraint domain> rejects the branch immediately; stronger <constraint propagation>, such as <arc consistency>, can detect additional incompatibilities among future choices.

This combines early rejection with depth-first storage. It retains the current assignment and domain changes rather than the entire breadth-first layer of partial placements. A direct knight-variable implementation uses $O(kn^2)$ space for explicit domains, plus its assignment and restoration trail, instead of an exponentially large <breadth-first search> <search frontier>. Propagation has a computational cost and does not guarantee improvement on every input, but often prevents exploration of vast inconsistent subtrees.

The **<minimum remaining values heuristic>** selects an unassigned <variable> with the fewest currently legal values, exposing the likeliest failure first. The **<degree heuristic>** selects a <variable> constrained to the largest number of other unassigned <variables>, often breaking <minimum remaining values heuristic> ties. Degree is measured in the remaining <constraint graph>, not by counting assigned neighbors. In the symmetric knight-variable encoding the remaining degrees can all tie, so the heuristic need not discriminate there.<CS 188 constraint ordering and structure notes>[§2.4, Minimum Remaining Values: choose an unassigned variable with the fewest valid remaining values]{ref}

=== d
{scope}

==== Solution

A useful <local search> state is any occupied-square <subset> $S\subseteq Q$, allowing temporary attacks. Let $C(S)$ count unordered attacking pairs and $N=n^2$. Maximize
$$
\boxed{F(S)=|S|-(N+1)C(S).}
$$
ACTIONS can offer insertion at an empty square, deletion of an occupied square, and relocation of one knight to an empty square. RESULT is respectively $S\cup\{q\}$, $S\setminus\{p\}$, and $(S\setminus\{p\})\cup\{q\}$. Insertions and deletions alone connect the whole <state space>; relocation is a useful additional proposal.

Every conflicting state has $F(S)\leq N-(N+1)<0$, whereas the empty board has score zero. Every conflict-free state has score exactly its knight count. Hence **every global maximizer is a maximum-size nonattacking placement**. If a knight participates in $r\geq1$ conflicts, deleting it changes the score by $-1+(N+1)r>0$, so a positive-conflict local stopping point cannot persist when improving deletions are considered.

From a random board, choose score-improving moves, with random restarts or occasional non-improving proposals to escape poor conflict-free local optima. A maximal placement is not necessarily a maximum placement: accepting only legal insertions can get stuck too early. This <objective function> defines the intended optimization precisely; a finite heuristic run still gives no general global-optimality certificate.

=== e
{scope}

==== Solution

**The depth assertion is false.** Each move occupies one new square, so the general bound is $n^2$, not $n$. On a $2\times2$ board no knights attack, and a play has four moves. More generally, filling a single checkerboard colour gives a legal partial play of length $\lceil n^2/2\rceil$, since knight moves always change colour.

**The opponent-independent “best possible move” assertion is false.** <Minimax search> maximizes the guaranteed <utility> against an opponent choosing the worst continuation. It need not maximize the outcome against a particular weaker opponent. If a winning <strategy> is available, its guaranteed win cannot be improved on in win/loss utility; if every root move loses against perfect play, all have the same minimax value, yet some may exploit a weaker opponent better. Thus the valid universal statement is that a minimax <strategy> guarantees its minimax value, not that its root choice is best for every possible opponent behavior.<CS 188 minimax notes>[§3.2, opening assumption: the opponent chooses the move worst for the agent]{ref}

**The $O(n)$ storage assertion is false for the usual minimax implementation.** Here the maximum <branching factor> is $O(n^2)$ and maximum depth is $O(n^2)$. A conventional <depth-first search> retaining pending successors uses the usual $O(bm)=O(n^4)$ search-node bound. Generating successors lazily and applying/undoing moves in one board can reduce this to $O(n^2)$ stack frames plus board storage; neither accounting should confuse the board side length $n$ with its $n^2$ squares.

**The winning-strategy assertion is true.** The game is finite, has perfect information, alternates two players, and has no draw under the <normal-play convention>. A terminal position is losing for the player to move. By <backward induction>, a nonterminal position is winning exactly when at least one legal move reaches a losing position; otherwise it is losing. This labels every position, including the initial board, so exactly one of the two players has a winning <strategy>.

== 3
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

Write $\tau$ for the table. The positive initial <planning fluents> read from the original figure are
$$
\boxed{I=\{\operatorname{On}(A,\tau),\operatorname{On}(B,\tau),\operatorname{On}(C,A),\operatorname{Clear}(B),\operatorname{Clear}(C)\}.}
$$
The static type facts are $\operatorname{Block}(A)$, $\operatorname{Block}(B)$ and $\operatorname{Block}(C)$; the table is not a block. With an explicit table-availability <predicate>, take $\operatorname{Clear}(\tau)$ to be permanently true. Under the <closed-world assumption>, all other well-typed initial <planning fluents> are false; in particular, $A$ is not clear.

The goal is the partial-state <conjunction>
$$
\boxed{G=\operatorname{On}(A,B)\land\operatorname{On}(B,C).}
$$
It need not redundantly prescribe every final fact. With only these three blocks and a valid <blocks world> state, the completed stack also places $C$ on the table and leaves $A$ clear.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

Use direct-transfer <STRIPS> actions: one action includes picking up a block and putting it down again. There is no separate holding state. Ground $b,y$ as distinct blocks and $x$ as a support, either a block or the table, with $x\ne b$ and $x\ne y$.

For $\operatorname{Move}(b,x,y)$, take
$$
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Pre}&=\{\operatorname{Block}(b),\operatorname{Block}(y),\operatorname{On}(b,x),\operatorname{Clear}(b),\operatorname{Clear}(y)\},\\
\operatorname{Add}&=\{\operatorname{On}(b,y)\}\cup\bigl(\{\operatorname{Clear}(x)\}\text{ if }x\ne\tau\bigr),\\
\operatorname{Del}&=\{\operatorname{On}(b,x),\operatorname{Clear}(y)\}.
\end{aligned}
$$
The <preconditions> ensure that $b$ can be picked up and that the destination has room. Moving it frees a block source and occupies the destination, while $\operatorname{Clear}(b)$ persists. When $x=\tau$, table availability remains true; its redundant addition is simply omitted. Since $x$ is fixed in each <ground action>, this case distinction constructs ordinary add lists rather than introducing conditional action effects. The <search transition model> is **$(S\setminus\operatorname{Del})\cup\operatorname{Add}$**.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

Ground $b,x$ as distinct blocks. For $\operatorname{MoveToTable}(b,x)$, take
$$
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Pre}&=\{\operatorname{Block}(b),\operatorname{Block}(x),\operatorname{On}(b,x),\operatorname{Clear}(b)\},\\
\operatorname{Add}&=\{\operatorname{On}(b,\tau),\operatorname{Clear}(x)\},\\
\operatorname{Del}&=\{\operatorname{On}(b,x)\}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus a clear block leaves its block support, makes that support clear, and joins the unlimited table. **$\operatorname{Clear}(b)$ persists, and table availability is never deleted.** Requiring $x$ to be a block excludes a redundant table-to-table action. The static <predicates> need not be stored as time-varying <planning fluents>.

=== d
{scope}

==== Solution

Let $G_1=\operatorname{On}(A,B)$ and $G_2=\operatorname{On}(B,C)$. The familiar obstruction is the <Sussman anomaly> for restricted <goal-by-goal planning>.

If one first constructs the obvious isolated plan for $G_1$, it moves $C$ off $A$ and then puts $A$ on $B$. Now $B$ is not clear, so achieving $G_2$ requires taking $A$ off $B$, undoing $G_1$. If one first achieves the isolated $G_2$ directly, it puts $B$ on $C$ while $C$ is still on $A$. To free $A$, it must move $C$, which first requires moving $B$ off $C$, undoing $G_2$. Therefore neither ordering of those independent subplans works while protecting the first achieved goal.

**The literal concatenation-only definition in the question is too weak to imply the requested impossibility.** A valid <plan> is
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{MoveToTable}(C,A),\quad\operatorname{Move}(B,\tau,C),\quad\operatorname{Move}(A,\tau,B).}
$$
The first two actions constitute a <plan> for $G_2$ from the original state, and the last action is a <plan> for $G_1$ from the resulting state, preserving $G_2$. Thus this is itself a concatenation of the stated kind. Its crucial first action prepares for the later subgoal before completing $G_2$.

\Image[artificial-intelligence-plan-supports.png]
{title=Support relations along the derived three-action <blocks world> plan}

The green-outlined cells mark achieved goal facts. This original solution timeline makes the distinction explicit: **independent goal-directed subplanning fails, but unrestricted concatenation does not**. To obtain the intended impossibility claim, “non-interleaved” must also forbid anticipatory preparation or require the isolated subplans described above. No source input is missing; the issue is the breadth of the printed definition.

=== e
{scope}

==== Solution

Use a sequential <SATPlan> encoding with times $t=0,\ldots,T$. The fluent universe is
$$
\mathcal P=\{\operatorname{On}(b,x):b\in\{A,B,C\},\ x\in\{A,B,C,\tau\}\setminus\{b\}\}\cup\{\operatorname{Clear}(A),\operatorname{Clear}(B),\operatorname{Clear}(C)\}.
$$
There are nine support <Boolean variables> and three clearance <Boolean variables> per time layer. Static block types, parameter inequalities and permanent table availability are handled during <action grounding>. For each well-typed <ground action> $a$ from parts (b)–(c), introduce an occurrence <Boolean variable> $a_t$ for $0\leq t<T$. Allow **at most one action per layer**, including no action, so that a horizon of $T$ encodes at most $T$ physical actions.

The complete initial-state formula is
$$
\begin{aligned}
I_0={}&\operatorname{On}(A,\tau)_0\land\operatorname{On}(B,\tau)_0\land\operatorname{On}(C,A)_0\\
&\land\operatorname{Clear}(B)_0\land\operatorname{Clear}(C)_0\land\neg\operatorname{Clear}(A)_0\\
&\land\neg\operatorname{On}(A,B)_0\land\neg\operatorname{On}(A,C)_0\\
&\land\neg\operatorname{On}(B,A)_0\land\neg\operatorname{On}(B,C)_0\\
&\land\neg\operatorname{On}(C,B)_0\land\neg\operatorname{On}(C,\tau)_0.
\end{aligned}
$$
Every well-typed fluent not initially true is explicitly false. The final goal formula is simply **$G_T=\operatorname{On}(A,B)_T\land\operatorname{On}(B,C)_T$**. Leaving unrelated final facts unconstrained is appropriate; leaving unrelated initial facts unconstrained would introduce imaginary starting states.

For each $t<T$, include all <action precondition axioms> $a_t\to\bigwedge_{p\in\operatorname{Pre}(a)}p_t$, after removing static true conditions. For example,
$$
\operatorname{Move}(A,\tau,B)_t\to\operatorname{On}(A,\tau)_t\land\operatorname{Clear}(A)_t\land\operatorname{Clear}(B)_t.
$$
The <precondition> axiom for $\operatorname{MoveToTable}(C,A)_t$ similarly requires $\operatorname{On}(C,A)_t\land\operatorname{Clear}(C)_t$.

For every fluent $p\in\mathcal P$, let $\operatorname{Add}_p(t)$ be the <logical disjunction> of actions at $t$ adding $p$, and $\operatorname{Del}_p(t)$ the <logical disjunction> deleting it. Include the <successor-state axiom>
$$
p_{t+1}\leftrightarrow\operatorname{Add}_p(t)\lor\bigl(p_t\land\neg\operatorname{Del}_p(t)\bigr).
$$
It combines action effects with the <frame axiom>, preventing unsupported changes. A fully grounded example is
$$
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{On}(A,B)_{t+1}\leftrightarrow{}&\operatorname{Move}(A,\tau,B)_t\lor\operatorname{Move}(A,C,B)_t\\
&\lor\bigl(\operatorname{On}(A,B)_t\land\neg\operatorname{Move}(A,B,C)_t\land\neg\operatorname{MoveToTable}(A,B)_t\bigr).
\end{aligned}
$$
Apply the same construction to every support and clearance fluent, using the exact add/delete lists. If no action occurs, both effect <logical disjunctions> are false, so all fluents persist.

The sequential <action exclusion axioms> are $\neg a_t\lor\neg b_t$ for every pair of distinct grounded actions $a,b$. For example,
$$
\neg\operatorname{Move}(A,\tau,B)_t\lor\neg\operatorname{Move}(C,A,B)_t
$$
forbids selecting those two occurrences together. Do not add a requirement that some action occur in every layer: that would remove idle padding and change “at most $T$” into an exact-length requirement. One may additionally include true <planning state invariants>, such as a block having at most one support and a block support carrying at most one block; the unlimited table must be exempt from the latter restriction.

Finally, conjoin $I_0$, $G_T$, and all precondition, successor and exclusion formulas over the layers to obtain $\varphi_T$. It is a <propositional formula>; if a solver requires <conjunctive normal form>, use a satisfiability-preserving <Tseitin transformation> with auxiliary <Boolean variables>. The equivalence between this encoding and real action <sequences> follows from <sequential SATPlan correctness>: a satisfying assignment induces exactly the stated <STRIPS> trajectory, and every sufficiently short valid <plan> supplies such an assignment.

=== f
{scope}

==== Solution

Read the satisfying assignment in increasing time order. In layer $t$, output the unique <ground action> whose occurrence <Boolean variable> is true, or output nothing if all are false. Discard idle layers. The result is a <sequence> of at most $T$ actions.

For correctness, the layer-zero <planning fluents> are exactly the actual initial state. The <action precondition axioms> make the selected action applicable, while the <successor-state axioms> give precisely its add/delete update; the <action exclusion axioms> prevent simultaneous competing actions. By <induction>, every following time layer is the real state reached by the decoded prefix. The final goal formula therefore proves that **the extracted <plan> reaches both required goals**.

Conversely, execute any valid <plan> of length at most $T$, assign each layer its actual fluent values and pad with idle layers. These values satisfy every component of $\varphi_T$. In this instance, decoding the three-action trajectory in part (d) gives a model at $T=3$ and at every larger horizon after padding; direct state-space and propositional checks find no model for $T<3$.
